processes the original prompt plus the proposed tokens in a single forward pass and computes its own probabilities for every position in that sequence. This lets the target model verify multiple candidate tokens at once instead of generating them one by one, which is the usual bottleneck in autoregressive decoding.\n\nThe key correctness property is that the final output distribution can be made identical to ordinary target-model decoding. For each proposed token, the algorithm compares the draft probability with the target probability. If the target model strongly agrees, the token is accepted. If it disagrees, the token is rejected and a new token is resampled from a corrected distribution that favors the target model\u2019s probabilities while accounting for the draft\u2019s proposal. This rejection-sampling step preserves the target model\u2019s marginal distribution over generated text, so the method is not merely an approximation.\n\nIn practice, the speedup depends on how many draft tokens are accepted before a rejection, the relative cost of the draft and target models, and the efficiency of the target model\u2019s parallel verification. A good draft model that matches the target model\u2019s preferences can accept several tokens per target pass, reducing the number of expensive target forward passes. The speedup is limited when the draft model is too small or poorly aligned, causing frequent rejections, and when the target model\u2019s verification pass is not much cheaper per token than sequential decoding, especially on small batches or hardware where parallelism is inefficient.\"\n\nCount words. Let", "top2": [[[1596, -0.00223672017455101], [760, -6.796241283416748]], [[1144, -1.3113108252582606e-05], [4087, -11.872686386108398]], [[4087, -0.16675671935081482], [310, -1.882346272468567]], [[1156, -0.008090472780168056], [303, -4.864291667938232]], [[579, -0.26901891827583313], [25, -1.7353543043136597]], [[1622, -0.01070516835898161], [3296, -4.923411369